  "content": {
    "id": "p5-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Find the equation of the smallest circle that has points $(1,1)$ and $(7,9)$ on its perimeter.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(x-4)^2+(y-5)^2=5^2$.\n\nThe two points are endpoints of a diameter of the smallest circle.",
      "worked": "A chord cannot be longer than the diameter. The smallest circle therefore has the two points as endpoints of a diameter. Its centre is their midpoint:\n$$C=\\left(\\frac{1+7}{2},\\frac{1+9}{2}\\right)=(4,5).$$\nThe radius is the distance from $C$ to either endpoint:\n$$\\begin{align*}r&=\\sqrt{(4-1)^2+(5-1)^2}\\\\r&=\\sqrt{9+16}\\\\r&=5.\\end{align*}$$\nHence,\n$$(x-4)^2+(y-5)^2=5^2.$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 76
  },
